WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.685 --> 00:00:01.912
- [Ben] What is food?

00:00:01.912 --> 00:00:03.549
Food is any nutritious substance

00:00:03.549 --> 00:00:05.837
that people or animals eat or drink

00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:10.115
or that plants absorb in order
to maintain life and growth.

00:00:10.115 --> 00:00:11.928
My name is Ben Brown, and I'm a filmmaker,

00:00:11.928 --> 00:00:15.053
working with Great Big Story.

00:00:15.053 --> 00:00:17.496
I've always taken an
interest in nutrition.

00:00:17.496 --> 00:00:20.184
But, over the last few years,
I've been asking myself:

00:00:20.184 --> 00:00:22.669
what does food really mean to me?

00:00:22.669 --> 00:00:24.355
Every day, we are confronted with endless,

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:25.743
conflicting information,

00:00:25.743 --> 00:00:28.312
about what we should,
and should not be eating.

00:00:28.312 --> 00:00:32.479
Sugar-free, low-fat, GMO,
MSG, naturally-added flavors.

00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:35.069
As convenience in price

00:00:35.069 --> 00:00:37.181
take priority over quality and health,

00:00:37.181 --> 00:00:38.931
we find ourselves becoming disconnected

00:00:38.931 --> 00:00:41.480
from the food that sustains us.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.559
In search of some simple answers,
I traveled to Costa Rica,

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:47.816
to meet a farmer who abandoned
the fast pace city life,

00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:50.717
to reconnect with food,
by growing it himself.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:51.645
- [Henry] I'm Henry.

00:00:51.645 --> 00:00:53.096
Henry Karczynski.

00:00:53.096 --> 00:00:55.501
I'm manager of Villa Vanilla,

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:58.275
a organic, biodynamic spice farm.

00:00:58.275 --> 00:01:02.339
I grow spices here, in the
central pacific of Costa Rica.

00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:04.101
I started in Chicago.

00:01:04.101 --> 00:01:05.256
I grew up in Chicago.

00:01:05.256 --> 00:01:06.771
I had a couple jobs.

00:01:06.771 --> 00:01:08.424
I remember getting in my car,

00:01:08.424 --> 00:01:12.147
you know, I had to visit clients
on the north side of Chicago.

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:14.557
I would, like, go right to the drive-in,

00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:17.128
get my Big Mac, and french
fries, and soft drink,

00:01:17.128 --> 00:01:19.494
and then, head out on the
roads to my next contract.

00:01:19.494 --> 00:01:22.467
I mean, I would do that when I was 24.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.408
It was a rat race, and
everybody's trying to struggle

00:01:24.408 --> 00:01:25.368
to get up the ladder.

00:01:25.368 --> 00:01:26.888
And then, when they get
to the top, they go:

00:01:26.888 --> 00:01:28.061
what did I do with my life?

00:01:28.061 --> 00:01:31.560
You know, it's already too late.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.475
And I had to, sort of, restart, look
at what am I gonna do,

00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:38.067
and the little seed
started growing in me,

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.168
to actually do my own thing.

00:01:40.168 --> 00:01:43.795
I just had something growing
in me, to grow, to grow plants,

00:01:43.795 --> 00:01:46.611
to grow food, to do a farm.

00:01:46.611 --> 00:01:49.139
And, I was introduced to
biodynamics, from the book,

00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:50.877
the Secrets of the Soil.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:53.043
I think the most impact that had,

00:01:53.043 --> 00:01:55.818
was that it removed those blindfolds,

00:01:55.818 --> 00:01:58.985
and I was able to see the big picture.

00:02:00.115 --> 00:02:02.909
You know, in a way, a mission I have,

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:06.576
is to use the farm as
a, sort of, a message,

00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:10.208
for people to look at, at food.

00:02:11.075 --> 00:02:12.821
We are living on this planet,

00:02:12.821 --> 00:02:16.173
and so, we're also here
to enjoy the pleasures

00:02:16.173 --> 00:02:20.243
the food, the sensuality
of living on this planet.

00:02:20.243 --> 00:02:21.661
Food is a flavor.

00:02:21.661 --> 00:02:22.835
It's an aroma.

00:02:22.835 --> 00:02:24.835
It's something to enjoy.

00:02:26.653 --> 00:02:29.491
So, what I would actually recommend,

00:02:29.491 --> 00:02:32.179
to people that are starting
that first journeys,

00:02:32.179 --> 00:02:33.064
start a garden.

00:02:33.064 --> 00:02:34.600
Grow your tomatoes, grow your lettuce.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.253
Make that first step.

00:02:36.253 --> 00:02:39.228
You'll be blown away on how much,

00:02:39.228 --> 00:02:41.320
that how good that food tastes,

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.487
and you can be started,
connecting with Mother Earth.

00:02:46.525 --> 00:02:47.704
The most important thing, is to get that,

00:02:47.704 --> 00:02:48.877
take that first step.

00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:52.122
As I said, at one time, I
was a little bit discouraged,

00:02:52.122 --> 00:02:53.315
because of the environments.

00:02:53.315 --> 00:02:56.291
But recently, there's optimism.

00:02:56.291 --> 00:02:59.791
There's lights at that end of that tunnel.

00:03:01.619 --> 00:03:04.104
- [Ben Voiceover] I share
Henry's optimism for change.

00:03:04.104 --> 00:03:06.024
We already know about the
endless health benefits

00:03:06.024 --> 00:03:09.053
of eating natural, organic produce.

00:03:09.053 --> 00:03:10.936
But, I also believe that by improving

00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:12.525
our relationship with food,

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:15.772
we can reconnect with
this beautiful planet.

00:03:15.772 --> 00:03:18.509
To consume consciously, is to take action,

00:03:18.509 --> 00:03:20.525
against the destructive objectives

00:03:20.525 --> 00:03:23.437
of large-scale food production.

00:03:23.437 --> 00:03:27.448
Mother Earth is a beautiful,
biological system.

00:03:27.448 --> 00:03:29.933
So, we must keep it simple.

00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:33.038
Take responsibility, and work to protect

00:03:33.038 --> 00:03:35.597
the only home we have.

00:03:35.597 --> 00:03:38.180
(gentle music)

